{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P976fKZSbjcoANCyBdjVQp","type":"tool_result","content":"package worker\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\tif _, err := d.Store.StartAttempt(ctx, jb.sj.ID); err != nil {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.start_failed\", \"step\", \"start_attempt\", \"error\", err)\n\t\tjb.finish(ctx, step{}, nil, jb.cause(closeout.CauseNoProcess, \"could not start the job\", false))\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\ttimeout := jb.role.Timeout\n\tif timeout <= 0 {\n\t\ttimeout = defaultTimeout\n\t}\n\tjb.deadline = d.Now().Add(timeout)\n\tref := jb.cfg.SHA\n\tif jb.exists {\n\t\tref = localRef(jb.branch)\n\t}\n\tcur, ok := jb.agentStep(ctx, jb.sj.ID, ref, nil)\n\tif !ok {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\t// An existing branch with no new code goes straight to the tests.\n\thead, _, done := jb.round(ctx, cur)\n\tif done {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tres := closeout.WorkerResult{Tests: closeout.TestsNone}\n\tif jb.role.Tests != \"\" {\n\t\tvar done bool\n\t\tif res, done = jb.testLoop(ctx, head); done {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif !jb.openPR(ctx, &res) {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tjb.finish(ctx, jb.final, &res, closeout.Cause{})\n}\n\n// agentStep runs an agent (or fix) step on ref. ok is false when the job has\n// been finished or stopped.\nfunc (jb *job) agentStep(ctx context.Context, id, ref string, extra []executor.File) (step, bool) {\n\tn := jb.is.Number\n\tej := jb.execJob(id, ref, jb.stepTimeout())\n\tif !jb.role.Network {\n\t\tej.Network = executor.NetworkAdvisory\n\t}\n\tej.Inputs = append(append([]executor.File(nil), jb.inputs...), extra...)\n\tej.Scrub = []string{agent.RoleFilePath(n, stageWorker, jb.role.Name)}\n\tuntrusted := append([]string(nil), jb.untrusted...)\n\tfor _, f := range extra {\n\t\tuntrusted = append(untrusted, f.Path)\n\t}\n\t// B: max_budget_usd caps the whole job. Each step gets what is left.\n\tif budget := jb.budgetCap(); budget > 0 {\n\t\tleft := budget - jb.cost\n\t\tif left <= 0 {\n\t\t\tjb.log.Warn(\"worker.budget_exhausted\", \"cap_usd\", budget, \"spent_usd\", jb.cost)\n\t\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, jb.final, \"\", jb.cause(closeout.CauseNoProcess, \"job budget exhausted (max_budget_usd)\", false))\n\t\t\treturn step{}, false\n\t\t}\n\t\tjb.stepBudget = left\n\t} else {\n\t\tjb.stepBudget = 0\n\t}\n\ts, cause, stopped := jb.runStep(ctx, ej, func(h executor.Handle) (executor.Command, error) {\n\t\t// A: plan.md is passed only when triage wrote it.\n\t\tuntrusted = append(untrusted, jb.triageInputs(h.Worktree)...)\n\t\treturn jb.agentCommand(h, untrusted)\n\t}, true, true)\n\tif stopped {\n\t\treturn step{}, false\n\t}\n\tif cause != nil {\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", *cause)\n\t\treturn step{}, false\n\t}\n\t// s is an agent or fix step here, never a test step.\n\tif jb.l.d.Readings != nil && len(s.res.Windows) > 0 && s.h.JobID != \"\" {\n\t\tjb.l.d.Readings.Observe(ctx, s.res.Windows)\n\t}\n\tif u, ok := reaper.StepUsageOf(s.res); ok && s.h.JobID != \"\" {\n\t\tjb.usage = append(jb.usage, stepUsage{s.h.JobID, u})\n\t\tjb.recordUsage(ctx, jb.usage[len(jb.usage)-1])\n\t}\n\tif s.res.CostUSD != nil {\n\t\tjb.cost += *s.res.CostUSD\n\t} else {\n\t\tjb.log.Warn(\"worker.no_cost_reported\", \"step\", id)\n\t}\n\tjb.log.Info(\"worker.step\", \"id\", id, \"kind\", \"agent\", \"exit\", s.st.ExitCode, \"state\", string(s.st.State))\n\tif jb.abnormal(ctx, s.st) {\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", closeout.Cause{})\n\t\treturn step{}, false\n\t}\n\tif s.st.ExitCode != 0 {\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", closeout.Cause{})\n\t\treturn step{}, false\n\t}\n\t// This step supersedes the earlier agent worktrees.\n\tfor _, h := range jb.live {\n\t\tif h.JobID != s.h.JobID {\n\t\t\tjb.cleanup(ctx, h)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tjb.live = []executor.Handle{s.h}\n\tif s.h.LogPath != \"\" {\n\t\tjb.agentLogs = append(jb.agentLogs, s.h.LogPath)\n\t}\n\tjb.final = s\n\treturn s, true\n}\n\n// stepTimeout is the budget a step starts with: what is left of the job's time.\n28:func (t *tick) candidates(ctx context.Context) ([]candidate, []error) {\n46:\t\t\tGuards: []string{labels.ExecutorFailed, labels.NeedsHuman, labels.Hold},\n62:\t\t\tGuards: []string{labels.ExecutorFailed, labels.NeedsHuman, labels.Hold},\n95:func (t *tick) consider(ctx context.Context, role config.Role, n int) (candidate, bool, error) {\n454:func TestCreatePRFailure(t *testing.T) {\n457:\th.gh.FailNext(\"CreatePR\", errBoom)\ninternal/github/fake/fake.go:317:func (f *Fake) FailNext(method string, err error) {\n7:ard (automated repository development) makes a GitHub repo maintain itself: scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n15:- **Workers** — every 5 min; implement the plan on `worker/<name>-<issue#>-<slug>`, run tests, open a draft PR\n28:    G -->|\"ard:worker:ROLE\"| W[\"Worker<br/>branch, code, test, draft PR\"]\n43:Each arrow is a label the controller sets at close-out; dashed lines show the stages it runs. A worker that answers `needs-network` before it has pushed anything goes back to the triage engineer once (the edge from Worker to Triage); a reviewer's `changes` verdict sends the PR back to the worker, at most three times; once every review passes, the merger lands it.\n61:    Changes --> [*]: worker re-requested\n69:`:done` hands off to the next stage's request label, `:changes` sends the PR back to the worker, and a failed job waits until a human removes `ard:executor:failed`.\n75:| `ard:triage:<role>` | Lead (via controller) or human | This triage role is requested. A needs-network worker close-out requests the engineer this way, and the lead's close-out re-requests the other roles in a network round |\n76:| `ard:worker:<role>` | Triage gate (controller) | Plan is complete; ready to implement |\n80:| `ard:review:<role>:changes` | Controller on close-out | Reviewer found blocking issues; sends the PR back to the worker |\n86:Eight more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:p0`, `ard:p1`, `ard:p2` and `ard:p3` (priority, set by humans only; ard never sets one: unlabelled counts as P2, several count as the highest; see the job-pool paragraph), `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\n88:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n92:**Agents never touch labels.** The executor's close-out removes `:running`, adds `:done` (or `ard:executor:failed`), and posts the summary comment. `ard:pending` is removed when the lead finishes, so it means \"not yet triaged\" and nothing else. In a network round the bounce reverses part of that: the worker close-out removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running` and requests the engineer; the engineer's close-out removes `ard:triage:lead:done` and adds `ard:pending` before its own `:done`; the lead's close-out removes the `:done` of the roles it re-opens and requests them again. The gate does not run while the lead's `:done` is absent.\n107:    plan.md              # triage roles except the lead: each owns one `##` section, replaced in place on a re-run; the worker follows it\n111:    worker-engineer.md         # worker: what changed, test results, open issues\n115:      worker-engineer-<ts>.log   # one per agent step; test logs stay on the host\n120:**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, and `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\n131:| `worker:*`, `review:*` | its role file only |\n133:**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n135:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. The bounce is on in `serve` (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) and applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): nothing is pushed and no PR is opened, and close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human`, with one comment that lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The same applies when the bounce is off. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies; a test drives the whole path (bounce, re-run, gate, approval, worker, reviewers and the three negative cases) against fake agents with no network.\n137:**Network round re-run (#386).** A round is open while the newest bounced worker has no triage engineer `done` after it (job ID order). `Prepare` persists the round in the job record (`NetworkState.Round`, `Done`, `Rerun`), so `Build` stays pure and Resume replays the same plan. The engineer's first `done` in a round consumes it: its close-out clears `ard:triage:lead:done` (`Plan.Clear`, removed in the request phase before the requests are added, 404 counting as success) and adds `ard:pending`, so the lead runs first, and persists the other triage roles that had finished `done` (`Plan.Rerun`). The lead's close-out in that round re-requests those roles and clears their `:done` (roles no longer configured are skipped), so all finish before the gate. A human re-request of the engineer sees no open round and does not re-arm again. The engineer may declare `inputs:` (`path`, `provider` `human` or `worker`, optional https `url`), parsed from the redacted role file and stored in the plan. The gate checks each path (plain characters, clean and relative, not under `.ard/`, `.github/` or `.git`), reads main once per tick at one SHA through `ls-tree` (no content, regular blobs only, size capped, cached per SHA) and holds with a \"waiting for inputs\" comment listing each missing path and the capture-note format until every human-provided input exists. A `worker` input with a `network: false` worker role, a bad path or an oversized file ends `needs-human`. A network round always requires a fresh approval, even with `require_approval: false` and even when the engineer removed every network need: the \"ready for approval\" marker gains a `round=<n>`, and an earlier approval is stale because the engineer's new `:done` follows it. An approval given while inputs are missing still counts once they land. Label state machine: the engineer's `done` close-out removes `ard:triage:lead:done` and adds `ard:pending` before its own `:done`; the lead's close-out removes `:done` from each re-opened role and requests it again; the gate does not run while the lead's `:done` is absent. The gate records one `wait` gate event when it starts waiting for inputs and one `inputs-ready` event when they land; the daily status-issue digest shows a *Waiting for inputs* count of open issues whose newest gate event is the wait.\n139:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n143:The stage in a role file's name is the label stage (`triage`, `worker`, `review`; `config.Kind` and the role directory spell the last one `reviewer`), so a triage and a worker role with the same name never share a file.\n153:worker: engineer      # lead only: which worker role implements the plan\n161:network:            # workers only, with status needs-network: 1 to 10 entries (#384)\n166:fetched:              # workers only, optional: at most 50 (#300)\n180:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n182:**One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n184:**One committer for main.** (Concurrency rule, #411: the clone's lock is exclusive for worktree add/remove/prune and shared for fetches, so a lane's fetch never reads a half-added or half-removed worktree; commits and pushes take neither.) Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init --pr`'s (local mode writes only the working tree, #303): it commits only `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/ard-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n186:**Why `.ard/` stays on `main` (decided, #1).** An orphan `ard-state` branch is ruled out: worker and reviewer logs ride on PR branches, and a PR cannot carry files from unrelated history. The single committer, the `[skip ci]` marker and the path guard answer the usual objections. `[skip ci]` applies to main-branch bot commits only; commits on a PR branch never carry it, so CI always runs on them.\n248:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are always forced after any config, because findings that vanish are autoclosed: `--max-issues-per-linter=0 --max-same-issues=0` (no truncation), `--new=false --new-from-rev= --new-from-merge-base= --new-from-patch=` (a repo config asking for \"new issues only\" would report nothing on a clean checkout), `--issues-exit-code=0 --fix=false --path-mode=abs`, plus JSON output to a temp file. It fails closed: a golangci-lint exit other than 0, a `typecheck` issue (a compile error), a module needing a newer Go than the image's (`GOTOOLCHAIN=local`; toolchain switching is a follow-up), a path outside the repository or with a control character, a malformed baseline, and a missing `baseline` file all exit non-zero with no output. Because the image builds golangci-lint with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, a repo config's `goplugin` custom linter cannot load and fails the run. The fingerprint is `lint:<rule>:<path>:<msg>`. `<rule>` is the linter plus `/<code>` for a check code (`staticcheck/SA4000`, `gosec/G104`), or for `govet` and `revive` an analyzer name (`govet/printf`). `<msg>` drops the code, control characters and repeated whitespace, and replaces every standalone number with `N`, so line numbers and \"complexity 31\" versus \"32\" never move a fingerprint; a fingerprint over 512 bytes has its message cut by runes. Identical fingerprints in one file collapse into one finding listing every line; the known gap is that a new occurrence of an identical message in the same file is hidden while the old one is open or baselined (a count-aware baseline is a follow-up). The group is `<rule>:<directory>`, so with `group_size: 20` issues are 20 per rule and directory. Severity has two levels: `medium` for `govet`, `staticcheck/SA*`, `gosec*`, `bodyclose`, `nilerr`, `nilnesserr`, `rowserrcheck`, `sqlclosecheck` and `durationcheck`, `low` for everything else; golangci-lint's own severity is ignored. Messages, source lines and paths are untrusted: they are rendered only inside `text` fences with ``` neutralised, and inline code spans have backticks replaced. Every `//nolint` directive in a `.go` file is itself a finding (rule `nolint`, group `nolint:<directory>`, `low`), so a worker cannot close a lint issue by suppressing it; for the same reason the starter protects `**/.golangci.{yml,yaml,toml,json}`. The baseline is the human-owned file `baseline` in the role directory (a protected path, so no agent can baseline a finding): `#` comments and blank lines are ignored, every other line is one fingerprint, and a finding whose fingerprint is listed is dropped. `ard init` does not ship one, so the scanner records a failure every 24 h until a human commits it; an empty file means no baseline. `scan --baseline` prints a baseline of all current findings (ignoring any existing one), and the exact Docker command is in the Dockerfile's header; the run logs how many baseline entries are unused. Baselining a finding that is open closes its issue as \"No longer detected\" (completed); to suppress it instead, close the issue as not planned. A golangci-lint version bump can change message text and so every fingerprint: bump the version and regenerate every repo's `baseline` in the same human PR.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"59f13bd1-4af6-4d3c-a6ca-3a44112790d5","uuid":"4de6a077-9961-4deb-a98c-4e5f2dbb38a5","timestamp":"2026-11-25T16:52:13.342Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"package worker\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\tif _, err := d.Store.StartAttempt(ctx, jb.sj.ID); err != nil {\n\t\tjb.log.Error(\"worker.start_failed\", \"step\", \"start_attempt\", \"error\", err)\n\t\tjb.finish(ctx, step{}, nil, jb.cause(closeout.CauseNoProcess, \"could not start the job\", false))\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\ttimeout := jb.role.Timeout\n\tif timeout <= 0 {\n\t\ttimeout = defaultTimeout\n\t}\n\tjb.deadline = d.Now().Add(timeout)\n\tref := jb.cfg.SHA\n\tif jb.exists {\n\t\tref = localRef(jb.branch)\n\t}\n\tcur, ok := jb.agentStep(ctx, jb.sj.ID, ref, nil)\n\tif !ok {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\t// An existing branch with no new code goes straight to the tests.\n\thead, _, done := jb.round(ctx, cur)\n\tif done {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tres := closeout.WorkerResult{Tests: closeout.TestsNone}\n\tif jb.role.Tests != \"\" {\n\t\tvar done bool\n\t\tif res, done = jb.testLoop(ctx, head); done {\n\t\t\treturn\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif !jb.openPR(ctx, &res) {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tjb.finish(ctx, jb.final, &res, closeout.Cause{})\n}\n\n// agentStep runs an agent (or fix) step on ref. ok is false when the job has\n// been finished or stopped.\nfunc (jb *job) agentStep(ctx context.Context, id, ref string, extra []executor.File) (step, bool) {\n\tn := jb.is.Number\n\tej := jb.execJob(id, ref, jb.stepTimeout())\n\tif !jb.role.Network {\n\t\tej.Network = executor.NetworkAdvisory\n\t}\n\tej.Inputs = append(append([]executor.File(nil), jb.inputs...), extra...)\n\tej.Scrub = []string{agent.RoleFilePath(n, stageWorker, jb.role.Name)}\n\tuntrusted := append([]string(nil), jb.untrusted...)\n\tfor _, f := range extra {\n\t\tuntrusted = append(untrusted, f.Path)\n\t}\n\t// B: max_budget_usd caps the whole job. Each step gets what is left.\n\tif budget := jb.budgetCap(); budget > 0 {\n\t\tleft := budget - jb.cost\n\t\tif left <= 0 {\n\t\t\tjb.log.Warn(\"worker.budget_exhausted\", \"cap_usd\", budget, \"spent_usd\", jb.cost)\n\t\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, jb.final, \"\", jb.cause(closeout.CauseNoProcess, \"job budget exhausted (max_budget_usd)\", false))\n\t\t\treturn step{}, false\n\t\t}\n\t\tjb.stepBudget = left\n\t} else {\n\t\tjb.stepBudget = 0\n\t}\n\ts, cause, stopped := jb.runStep(ctx, ej, func(h executor.Handle) (executor.Command, error) {\n\t\t// A: plan.md is passed only when triage wrote it.\n\t\tuntrusted = append(untrusted, jb.triageInputs(h.Worktree)...)\n\t\treturn jb.agentCommand(h, untrusted)\n\t}, true, true)\n\tif stopped {\n\t\treturn step{}, false\n\t}\n\tif cause != nil {\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", *cause)\n\t\treturn step{}, false\n\t}\n\t// s is an agent or fix step here, never a test step.\n\tif jb.l.d.Readings != nil && len(s.res.Windows) > 0 && s.h.JobID != \"\" {\n\t\tjb.l.d.Readings.Observe(ctx, s.res.Windows)\n\t}\n\tif u, ok := reaper.StepUsageOf(s.res); ok && s.h.JobID != \"\" {\n\t\tjb.usage = append(jb.usage, stepUsage{s.h.JobID, u})\n\t\tjb.recordUsage(ctx, jb.usage[len(jb.usage)-1])\n\t}\n\tif s.res.CostUSD != nil {\n\t\tjb.cost += *s.res.CostUSD\n\t} else {\n\t\tjb.log.Warn(\"worker.no_cost_reported\", \"step\", id)\n\t}\n\tjb.log.Info(\"worker.step\", \"id\", id, \"kind\", \"agent\", \"exit\", s.st.ExitCode, \"state\", string(s.st.State))\n\tif jb.abnormal(ctx, s.st) {\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", closeout.Cause{})\n\t\treturn step{}, false\n\t}\n\tif s.st.ExitCode != 0 {\n\t\tjb.finishWithPR(ctx, s, \"\", closeout.Cause{})\n\t\treturn step{}, false\n\t}\n\t// This step supersedes the earlier agent worktrees.\n\tfor _, h := range jb.live {\n\t\tif h.JobID != s.h.JobID {\n\t\t\tjb.cleanup(ctx, h)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tjb.live = []executor.Handle{s.h}\n\tif s.h.LogPath != \"\" {\n\t\tjb.agentLogs = append(jb.agentLogs, s.h.LogPath)\n\t}\n\tjb.final = s\n\treturn s, true\n}\n\n// stepTimeout is the budget a step starts with: what is left of the job's time.\n28:func (t *tick) candidates(ctx context.Context) ([]candidate, []error) {\n46:\t\t\tGuards: []string{labels.ExecutorFailed, labels.NeedsHuman, labels.Hold},\n62:\t\t\tGuards: []string{labels.ExecutorFailed, labels.NeedsHuman, labels.Hold},\n95:func (t *tick) consider(ctx context.Context, role config.Role, n int) (candidate, bool, error) {\n454:func TestCreatePRFailure(t *testing.T) {\n457:\th.gh.FailNext(\"CreatePR\", errBoom)\ninternal/github/fake/fake.go:317:func (f *Fake) FailNext(method string, err error) {\n7:ard (automated repository development) makes a GitHub repo maintain itself: scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n15:- **Workers** — every 5 min; implement the plan on `worker/<name>-<issue#>-<slug>`, run tests, open a draft PR\n28:    G -->|\"ard:worker:ROLE\"| W[\"Worker<br/>branch, code, test, draft PR\"]\n43:Each arrow is a label the controller sets at close-out; dashed lines show the stages it runs. A worker that answers `needs-network` before it has pushed anything goes back to the triage engineer once (the edge from Worker to Triage); a reviewer's `changes` verdict sends the PR back to the worker, at most three times; once every review passes, the merger lands it.\n61:    Changes --> [*]: worker re-requested\n69:`:done` hands off to the next stage's request label, `:changes` sends the PR back to the worker, and a failed job waits until a human removes `ard:executor:failed`.\n75:| `ard:triage:<role>` | Lead (via controller) or human | This triage role is requested. A needs-network worker close-out requests the engineer this way, and the lead's close-out re-requests the other roles in a network round |\n76:| `ard:worker:<role>` | Triage gate (controller) | Plan is complete; ready to implement |\n80:| `ard:review:<role>:changes` | Controller on close-out | Reviewer found blocking issues; sends the PR back to the worker |\n86:Eight more labels sit outside the grammar: `ard:p0`, `ard:p1`, `ard:p2` and `ard:p3` (priority, set by humans only; ard never sets one: unlabelled counts as P2, several count as the highest; see the job-pool paragraph), `ard:approved` (human go-ahead before a worker starts; counts only when a triage-or-higher collaborator added it after triage finished), `ard:tests:failed` (worker could not get tests green; set by worker close-out on the issue and the PR, cleared by a green run), `ard:keep` (hold failed-job resources past 24 h) and `ard:pause` (on the pinned status issue, stops all new claims). `ard labels sync` creates and updates the full set from the resolved config, idempotently, and never deletes a label: stale `ard:` labels are only reported. In `label` mode a custom `intake.label` is part of the catalogue: sync creates it with a description saying it admits the issue to triage, and switching back to the default leaves the old custom label reported as stale, never deleted. `ard labels sync` and `ard config check` reject an intake label that is one of ard's own label names, naming `intake.label`; `ard serve` logs `config.intake_invalid` once per distinct value. `--repo owner/name` names the repository; when omitted, it is read from the `origin` remote. Role names are at most 31 characters and scanner names at most 38, because GitHub caps a label at 50 characters. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so branch names stay unambiguous.\n88:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n92:**Agents never touch labels.** The executor's close-out removes `:running`, adds `:done` (or `ard:executor:failed`), and posts the summary comment. `ard:pending` is removed when the lead finishes, so it means \"not yet triaged\" and nothing else. In a network round the bounce reverses part of that: the worker close-out removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running` and requests the engineer; the engineer's close-out removes `ard:triage:lead:done` and adds `ard:pending` before its own `:done`; the lead's close-out removes the `:done` of the roles it re-opens and requests them again. The gate does not run while the lead's `:done` is absent.\n107:    plan.md              # triage roles except the lead: each owns one `##` section, replaced in place on a re-run; the worker follows it\n111:    worker-engineer.md         # worker: what changed, test results, open issues\n115:      worker-engineer-<ts>.log   # one per agent step; test logs stay on the host\n120:**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, and `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\n131:| `worker:*`, `review:*` | its role file only |\n133:**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\n135:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. The bounce is on in `serve` (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) and applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): nothing is pushed and no PR is opened, and close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human`, with one comment that lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The same applies when the bounce is off. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies; a test drives the whole path (bounce, re-run, gate, approval, worker, reviewers and the three negative cases) against fake agents with no network.\n137:**Network round re-run (#386).** A round is open while the newest bounced worker has no triage engineer `done` after it (job ID order). `Prepare` persists the round in the job record (`NetworkState.Round`, `Done`, `Rerun`), so `Build` stays pure and Resume replays the same plan. The engineer's first `done` in a round consumes it: its close-out clears `ard:triage:lead:done` (`Plan.Clear`, removed in the request phase before the requests are added, 404 counting as success) and adds `ard:pending`, so the lead runs first, and persists the other triage roles that had finished `done` (`Plan.Rerun`). The lead's close-out in that round re-requests those roles and clears their `:done` (roles no longer configured are skipped), so all finish before the gate. A human re-request of the engineer sees no open round and does not re-arm again. The engineer may declare `inputs:` (`path`, `provider` `human` or `worker`, optional https `url`), parsed from the redacted role file and stored in the plan. The gate checks each path (plain characters, clean and relative, not under `.ard/`, `.github/` or `.git`), reads main once per tick at one SHA through `ls-tree` (no content, regular blobs only, size capped, cached per SHA) and holds with a \"waiting for inputs\" comment listing each missing path and the capture-note format until every human-provided input exists. A `worker` input with a `network: false` worker role, a bad path or an oversized file ends `needs-human`. A network round always requires a fresh approval, even with `require_approval: false` and even when the engineer removed every network need: the \"ready for approval\" marker gains a `round=<n>`, and an earlier approval is stale because the engineer's new `:done` follows it. An approval given while inputs are missing still counts once they land. Label state machine: the engineer's `done` close-out removes `ard:triage:lead:done` and adds `ard:pending` before its own `:done`; the lead's close-out removes `:done` from each re-opened role and requests it again; the gate does not run while the lead's `:done` is absent. The gate records one `wait` gate event when it starts waiting for inputs and one `inputs-ready` event when they land; the daily status-issue digest shows a *Waiting for inputs* count of open issues whose newest gate event is the wait.\n139:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n143:The stage in a role file's name is the label stage (`triage`, `worker`, `review`; `config.Kind` and the role directory spell the last one `reviewer`), so a triage and a worker role with the same name never share a file.\n153:worker: engineer      # lead only: which worker role implements the plan\n161:network:            # workers only, with status needs-network: 1 to 10 entries (#384)\n166:fetched:              # workers only, optional: at most 50 (#300)\n180:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n182:**One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n184:**One committer for main.** (Concurrency rule, #411: the clone's lock is exclusive for worktree add/remove/prune and shared for fetches, so a lane's fetch never reads a half-added or half-removed worktree; commits and pushes take neither.) Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init --pr`'s (local mode writes only the working tree, #303): it commits only `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/ard-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n186:**Why `.ard/` stays on `main` (decided, #1).** An orphan `ard-state` branch is ruled out: worker and reviewer logs ride on PR branches, and a PR cannot carry files from unrelated history. The single committer, the `[skip ci]` marker and the path guard answer the usual objections. `[skip ci]` applies to main-branch bot commits only; commits on a PR branch never carry it, so CI always runs on them.\n248:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are always forced after any config, because findings that vanish are autoclosed: `--max-issues-per-linter=0 --max-same-issues=0` (no truncation), `--new=false --new-from-rev= --new-from-merge-base= --new-from-patch=` (a repo config asking for \"new issues only\" would report nothing on a clean checkout), `--issues-exit-code=0 --fix=false --path-mode=abs`, plus JSON output to a temp file. It fails closed: a golangci-lint exit other than 0, a `typecheck` issue (a compile error), a module needing a newer Go than the image's (`GOTOOLCHAIN=local`; toolchain switching is a follow-up), a path outside the repository or with a control character, a malformed baseline, and a missing `baseline` file all exit non-zero with no output. Because the image builds golangci-lint with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, a repo config's `goplugin` custom linter cannot load and fails the run. The fingerprint is `lint:<rule>:<path>:<msg>`. `<rule>` is the linter plus `/<code>` for a check code (`staticcheck/SA4000`, `gosec/G104`), or for `govet` and `revive` an analyzer name (`govet/printf`). `<msg>` drops the code, control characters and repeated whitespace, and replaces every standalone number with `N`, so line numbers and \"complexity 31\" versus \"32\" never move a fingerprint; a fingerprint over 512 bytes has its message cut by runes. Identical fingerprints in one file collapse into one finding listing every line; the known gap is that a new occurrence of an identical message in the same file is hidden while the old one is open or baselined (a count-aware baseline is a follow-up). The group is `<rule>:<directory>`, so with `group_size: 20` issues are 20 per rule and directory. Severity has two levels: `medium` for `govet`, `staticcheck/SA*`, `gosec*`, `bodyclose`, `nilerr`, `nilnesserr`, `rowserrcheck`, `sqlclosecheck` and `durationcheck`, `low` for everything else; golangci-lint's own severity is ignored. Messages, source lines and paths are untrusted: they are rendered only inside `text` fences with ``` neutralised, and inline code spans have backticks replaced. Every `//nolint` directive in a `.go` file is itself a finding (rule `nolint`, group `nolint:<directory>`, `low`), so a worker cannot close a lint issue by suppressing it; for the same reason the starter protects `**/.golangci.{yml,yaml,toml,json}`. The baseline is the human-owned file `baseline` in the role directory (a protected path, so no agent can baseline a finding): `#` comments and blank lines are ignored, every other line is one fingerprint, and a finding whose fingerprint is listed is dropped. `ard init` does not ship one, so the scanner records a failure every 24 h until a human commits it; an empty file means no baseline. `scan --baseline` prints a baseline of all current findings (ignoring any existing one), and the exact Docker command is in the Dockerfile's header; the run logs how many baseline entries are unused. Baselining a finding that is open closes its issue as \"No longer detected\" (completed); to suppress it instead, close the issue as not planned. A golangci-lint version bump can change message text and so every fingerprint: bump the version and regenerate every repo's `baseline` in the same human PR.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01P976fKZSbjcoANCyBdjVQp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
